Now the archive interior looks good — warm, detailed, shelves, tables, pendant lamps, NPC Mara. So the darkness was due to lights off in the frozen-camera test. Great.

Notable: the huge black rectangular mass on the right foreground (foreground occlusion) is likely a wall/counter very close to the camera? Camera at x=-16.9 near the east wall/door; on the right is the doorway jamb/wall black. On the left, shelves. It's fine, though the right black mass occupies 1/3 of the screen; that's the door frame. In actual third-person, the camera won't be exactly at the doorway.

Interior looks good. The "TRANSIT ARCHIVE" label floats and is legible. Player's back visible. Great.

Now, the requirement "prevent interior walls or exterior views from appearing transparent through the camera" — in this shot, no transparency. Good.

Now back to performance. We improved spawn to 622 calls, street 1127. Can we do more easy wins? The biggest dynamic meshes now are NPCs (~19 each × ~9 = 171), cats 19×6=114, birds 20×~6=120?, player 32. Could reduce cat/bird draw calls using batchPart on their parts (already done — earlier we added batchPart for bird/cat). 19 per cat is still high because each part (body, head, legs, tail) has one merged mesh per material, plus eyes. Hmm.

We can reduce by merging more aggressively with vertex colors within each articulated part. For each rig part, we currently merge per material. Characters have many materials: skin, hair, cloth, pants, shoes, accent, eyes, white, black, iris, pupil. Each part (head) has ~9 materials → ~9 draw calls. If we merged per part into one mesh with vertex colors, we'd need a single material with vertexColors and maybe emissive not needed. This could cut character draw calls 3-4×. But implementing vertex-color merging requires converting each material's color to vertex colors and handling MeshToonMaterial. Shading is toon with same gradient map; emissive accents on characters? Most character materials are toon colors and eyes; no emissive except maybe none. We could merge all opaque toon parts of a character part into one geometry with a single vertex-colored MeshToonMaterial. This is doable:

```
function bakeColor(geo, color) { add color attribute per vertex }
```
Need a shared `vertexToonMat = new MeshToonMaterial({vertexColors:true, gradientMap:toonRamp})`. For each mesh bucket part, convert geometry to non-indexed? Vertex colors can be added to indexed too (per vertex). Clone geometry, applyMatrix4, set color attribute. Merge all geos regardless of material; but must only merge geometries with same attributes (position, normal, uv, color). Add color to all. Some geometries have uv, some don't (BoxGeometry has uv; Sphere has uv; all have uv). Capsule has uv. Good. Also morph? none.

But materials can have different `emissive` or `transparent` (eyes white/black are opaque). Hair etc. All toon materials with gradientMap; some may have emissive accents (accessory neon). Let's check character material creation: all `toon(color)` with no extra except maybe eyes. So safe within a character rig part. But hands/fingers etc. Also any MeshStandard parts? Shoes use toon. All toon.

For each part, instead of batching per material, bake vertex colors into one geometry and one mesh. That reduces each character part to 1-2 meshes (some transparent? beards? no). Player 32 → maybe 12 (body/head/legs/arms groups, plus backpack etc.). NPCs 19 → ~10 (head/body/2 legs/2 arms, each 1). Cats/birds similar. Save maybe 200-300 draw calls in dense views.

Risk: vertex colors + toon material: `MeshToonMaterial` supports vertexColors. We'd set material.color white, vertexColors true. Emissive effects lost (characters have accent scarf etc. no emissive). Eyes rely on exact colors; baked colors preserve hue; but sRGB? `material.color` is stored linear internally; `new THREE.Color(hex)` converts sRGB to linear (with color management). Setting vertex colors: we need to use the linear color values from material.color (already linear) → correct. The renderer treats vertex colors as linear. Good.

But beware materials with textures/transparency: eyes are opaque. Some parts use `toon(skin)` etc. All fine. However, `batchPart` currently buckets by material and preserves `castShadow`. Baking all into one mesh means castShadow true if any; fine.

Would this "lower quality"? Slightly: different materials could have different roughness/emissive, but toon characters only use color. Vertex-color interpolation across triangles is per-vertex constant per mesh, so flat colors preserved (each vertex gets its mesh color). No visual change except potential color-space mismatch.

This is a more invasive change. Is it worth it? The user asked to minimize lag. We already cut ~16% calls at spawn (677→622) and ~19% at street (1385→1127). Adding character merging could cut another ~200 calls in dense views (~15%). It's a reasonable optimization if done carefully. But risk of breaking character appearance (which we've carefully built) is real, and the user said don't lower quality. Character demos would reveal color shifts.

Alternative easier win: shadows. Only 56 shadow calls at perimeter. Not the bottleneck.

Another win: cull distant labels and fog wisps helps a bit.

Could reduce `renderer` antialias? That lowers quality.

Could set `renderer.setPixelRatio(min(dpr,1.5))` desktop; not much.

Adaptive DPR is the safest way to handle actual lag without touching art. Let's re-add it but with a lower threshold (only when sustained <42fps) and min 0.75. On capable hardware, no change. This directly addresses lag on weak devices, and preserves quality on good ones. The previous implementation was removed maybe due to light popping (perf.level tied to light budget). We'll decouple.

Also, one more perf improvement: `updateNearbyLights` currently sorts all local lights and enables 9. Sorting 66 lights per frame is cheap. Fine.

`drawMap()` at 10Hz redraws the whole map; at 2D canvas it's maybe 1-3ms. Could reduce to 6Hz. Not needed.

We should also verify audio and mission route again after batching changes, and ending. Then check git diff status (uncommitted).

